---
import LessonLayout from '../../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
const lessonRef = "CCC 1422-1498";
const lessonScripture = "Jn 20:21-23; Lk 15:11-32; 2 Cor 5:18-20; Jas 5:16";
---
<LessonLayout title="Penance and Reconciliation &mdash; Workbook" cic={lessonRef} scripture={lessonScripture}>

<section class="content">

<h2>Reviewing the Essentials</h2>

<ol>
<li>On what day did Christ institute the sacrament of Reconciliation?</li>
<li>What are the three acts of the penitent? Describe each in one sentence.</li>
<li>What does it mean that the priest acts <em>in persona Christi</em>?</li>
<li>What are the effects of the sacrament? List at least four.</li>
<li>What is an indulgence?</li>
</ol>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>For Reflection and Writing</h2>

<div class="question-block">
<p><strong>1.</strong> Recall your most recent confession. What was the most difficult part for you — the examination of conscience, the act of confessing, or believing you were truly forgiven? Why?</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p><strong>2.</strong> The father of the prodigal son ran toward his son before the son finished his apology. Does this image change the way you think about God's posture toward you when you sin? How?</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p><strong>3.</strong> Many people avoid confession because of shame. What is the difference between healthy shame (contrition) and unhealthy shame (despair)?</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p><strong>4.</strong> Think of someone you need to forgive. How might regularly receiving God's forgiveness in confession help you become a more forgiving person?</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p><strong>5.</strong> If a friend told you, "I don't need to confess to a priest; I can confess directly to God," how would you respond using what you learned from the Catechism?</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Practical Activities</h2>

<ol>
<li><strong>Examination of Conscience:</strong> Using the Ten Commandments, the Beatitudes, or a guide from a prayer app, do a thorough examination of conscience tonight. Write down what you find.</li>
<li><strong>Schedule a Confession:</strong> Look up confession times at a nearby parish and put one on your calendar this week. If it's been more than a year, make it a priority.</li>
<li><strong>Memorize the Act of Contrition:</strong> If you don't already know it by heart, practice this week until you can say it without reading.</li>
</ol>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Memory Verse</h2>

<blockquote>
<p>"Whose sins you forgive are forgiven them, and whose sins you retain are retained."</p>
<cite>John 20:23</cite>
</blockquote>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Prayer for This Week</h2>

<p>
Lord Jesus, you gave your Church the power to forgive sins in your name. Give me the courage to approach the sacrament of Reconciliation with an honest and humble heart. Help me trust not in my own worthiness but in your infinite mercy. May I receive your forgiveness with gratitude and share it generously with others. Through the intercession of Mary, Refuge of Sinners, may I never despair of your love.
</p>

<p><em>Amen.</em></p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<button class="print-button" onclick="window.print()">🖨️ Print Workbook</button>

</section>

</LessonLayout>
